    {
      "turn": 2,
      "doctor_question": "During that time, did you feel unusually euphoric or on top of the world?",
      "targeted_symptom": "S033",
      "patient_response": "Well, yes, I suppose euphoric is a good word for it. At first, anyway. I felt... oh, I felt almost glowing, really. It was like every single idea that popped into my head was just absolutely wonderful, pure gold. I remember standing in the living room looking at all those fabrics—the calicos and the little floral prints I'd pulled out of the attic—and just feeling this incredible warm rush, thinking, 'I am going to save the courthouse, and it's going to be magnificent.' I felt so capable, so alive, like I was finally doing exactly what I was meant to do for the town and everything was just clicking into place perfectly. \n\nBut then... well, you see, the trouble started when people didn't quite see the vision. If anyone questioned me—like Martha, she's the treasurer of the society, and she had the nerve to call and ask if maybe three quilts in a week was a bit too ambitious for our volunteer base—I just got incredibly snappish. I mean, unusually short-tempered for me. I snapped right back at her on the phone. I acted like she was deliberately standing in the way of progress, just dragging her feet to ruin my plans. I think I even told her that if she didn't want to help, she should just step aside because we had history to save and no time for doubters. I was just so intensely irritated that they couldn't keep up with how wonderful everything was, and it felt like they were all just intentionally trying to block me from getting this glorious project done.",
