    {
      "turn": 4,
      "doctor_question": "During that five-month period, were you ever hospitalized because of how energized or irritable you were?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Oh, no, no, I was never in the hospital for any of it. Goodness, the thought never even crossed my mind, and I don't think it crossed anyone else's either, at least not that they said to my face. I mean, where would I have even found the time to be hospitalized? I was much too busy! I had all those schedules, the fabric swatches, the caterers to wrangle... I suppose, looking back, people probably thought I was just being incredibly eccentric or maybe just a bit too passionate about local history. \n\nYou know, my neighbor, Susan—she lives right across the street, lovely woman, always keeps her petunias looking so nice—she did come over one afternoon when I was dragging a massive roll of batting out of my trunk. She asked if I was feeling quite alright because I was talking a mile a minute about the courthouse roof and how we were going to have a silent auction with a Victorian theme. But even she didn't suggest a doctor or a hospital or anything like that. She just brought me a casserole a few days later and told me to remember to breathe. \n\nSo, no, no hospital visits. I was just out there in the world, running around town, arguing with Martha at the bank, buying out the entire craft store's supply of rotary cutters... just living my life at a hundred miles an hour, completely unsupervised, if you can believe it. It's almost frightening to think about now, how I was just out there, spinning like a top for all those months, and nobody stepped in to stop me or take me to a clinic. But no, I managed to stay out of the hospital through the whole ordeal.",
