    {
      "turn": 5,
      "doctor_question": "Have you ever had a period lasting at least two weeks when you felt depressed or lost interest in almost everything?",
      "targeted_symptom": null,
      "patient_response": "Well, let me think about that... depressed or lost interest. You know, it's funny you ask it just like that, because after all that rushing around with the quilts and the historical society—after that whole five-month whirlwind I was just telling you about—I did crash, like I said. The bottom just fell out. But... was it two weeks? Was it depression? \n\nI don't really know if I'd call it being depressed, exactly. Or maybe I would? It's so hard to put a label on these things when you're the one living inside them. I mean, I remember sitting in my armchair in the living room—the blue floral one, right by the window where the afternoon sun comes in—and just looking at all those leftover fabric scraps in the corner. Piles and piles of calico and gingham that I had been so obsessed with just days before. And I just... I didn't care about them anymore. The thought of sewing another stitch made me feel almost physically heavy. \n\nBut as for it being a whole period of two weeks where I felt down or lost interest in everything... I'm really not sure. I mean, my daughter came to visit right around Easter, and we did go out to brunch at that little bistro on Main Street, and I think I enjoyed the mimosa and the eggs benedict. I don't remember feeling completely hopeless or anything like that. I was just so exhausted, you know? Like a balloon that had all the air let out of it. \n\nI stopped returning Martha's calls, of course, but that was mostly because I was too embarrassed about how I'd spoken to her before. And I stopped going to the society meetings. I just wanted to sit in my quiet house and watch the dust motes in the sunlight and not have to make any decisions about centerpieces or fundraising goals. But was it a true, deep depression that lasted for weeks on end? I really couldn't say for certain. It felt more like a terrible, lingering hangover from all that energy, just this profound tiredness where nothing seemed as shiny or important as it had before. So, I don't know if that counts as what you're asking about.",
