      "turn": 6,
      "doctor_question": "Before this five-month high-energy period, had you ever gone through a time when you felt depressed most of the day nearly every day for at least two weeks?",
      "targeted_symptom": "depressive_episode_duration",
      "patient_response": "Well, before that five-month period? No, no, not before. The timeline is... well, it’s all very specific in my mind because of how things played out with the historical society and the quilts. See, the really bad part—the part where I felt just completely awful every single day—that didn’t happen *before* the whirlwind. It happened *after*. \n\nIt started right around the time I realized what a mess I’d made of everything. The orders for the quilts had fallen completely behind because I’d promised far too many to far too many people, and the volunteer conflicts... oh, they just blew up spectacularly. Martha and I had that terrible row over the catering budget, and then Susan got involved, and before I knew it, half the committee wasn't speaking to me. And when I finally stopped moving fast enough to see the wreckage, that’s when it hit me. \n\nAnd it didn't just last two weeks. Heavens, no. I’ve been stuck in this incredibly low, heavy, guilty place ever since. It’s been... goodness, it’s been about nine months now of just feeling utterly weighed down by it all. Nine whole months of waking up and feeling this pit in my stomach about the quilts I never finished and the people I snapped at. I just sit in that blue armchair and think about how I ruined the gala, and the days just bleed into one another. So, no, not before the high energy. But after? Yes, for nine long months, just feeling completely stuck in the mud.",
